  cpp_preprocessor.pcpp - C++ integration compliance: preprocessor lines

  A line whose first token is # (or its digraph %:) is a C++ preprocessor
  line (BNF.md sec 10), copied as written: at module level it goes to the
  module's header, so every later line sees it; in a body it stays in
  place. A splice continues it on the next line.

  How to check, from repo\bin:
    pcpp res\tests\cpp_preprocessor.pcpp -r
    pcpp res\tests\cpp_preprocessor.pcpp -r -t x86_64-linux-gnu   (runs in WSL)
  EXPECT:
    two 2
    joined 12
    digraph 7
    in place 6
    [Heap] Allocs: 0, Frees: 0, Leaked: 0
  EXITCODE: 0
  The [Heap] line is the debug build's heap report.

  Break it: run each file below the same way, without -r; each reports
  exactly what the // comment on its marked line says, and nothing else:
    cpp_preprocessor_err_hash.pcpp
      a # that is not first on its line (LX0008)
*******************************************************************************/

module exe cpp_preprocessor;

#include <cstdlib>
#define KTWO 2
#define KJOINED \
  12
%:define KDIGRAPH 7

begin
  println("two {}", KTWO);
  println("joined {}", KJOINED);
  println("digraph {}", KDIGRAPH);
#define KLOCAL 6
  println("in place {}", KLOCAL);
end.
